WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Here’s the nightmare. The light turns green, it’s clear to go through.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.980
You make your way down the intersection, but out the corner of your eye…

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.120
*car crash sounds*

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:21.460
And an instant later, there’s another notch added to the 30,000 deaths from car crashes a year, of which up to 23% are intersection-related.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:29.740
So law enforcement and city officials all over the country have tried to solve this problem by installing red-light cameras

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.660
to get drivers to slow down, stop, and save lives rather than just blowing through red lights.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.540
"The ultimate hope is to improve safety at the intersection by reducing traffic crashses..."

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.760
There’s just one problem. These cameras don’t just stop car crashes, they create others as well.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:54.100
Since the 1960s, thousands of red light cameras have been posted around intersections across the US.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.580
And there’s no denying that these cameras really can and do save lives.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:06.760
But they also show an increase in less deadly rear end collisions because of drivers abruptly stopping to avoid red-light tickets.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:15.280
And depending on where cameras are placed, they create more crashes were there weren’t many or any at all to begin with.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.700
Crashes at intersections are most often caused by cars turning left at an intersection, while cars cross on the opposite side of the road.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.340
Many tickets are going to drivers who “California roll” to turn right on red.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.340
But those cars only make up only about 1% of  intersection crashes.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.800
There is, of course, another reason beyond safety why cities would install red light cameras.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:44.040
Tickets from red light cameras can cost a driver between $50 and $500.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:53.180
Cities like Orlando, Florida, Chicago, Illinois, and Washington, DC bring in million of dollars in fines from red light camera tickets.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:00.440
Camera vendors such as RedFlex Traffic Systems, American Traffic Solution, and several others have incentive to get a lot contracts because

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:07.920
they take care of the installation, operation, and maintenance of those cameras, which in turn cost cities millions of dollars.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:16.440
In Chicago, one ex-city official was convicted in receiving up to $2 million dollars in bribes for red light camera contracts

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:26.500
And in a lawsuit, a fired RedFlex executive named 13 other cities that allegedly participated in receiving bribes from the traffic camera company as well.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.200
If safety were really the biggest, or only, concern, there are alternatives to red light cameras

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:38.080
that would be just as effective and cost less—it would take just a bit more city planning.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:46.780
For example, the timing of yellow lights is key. The federal guidelines specify that yellow lights need only to fall between 3 and 6 seconds.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.680
But red light cameras might give cities incentive to stay at the shorter end of that range.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:03:03.800
In Florida, one intersection’s yellow light intervals were adjusted from 3.9 seconds to 4.8 seconds and there was a 79% drop in issued citations.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:10.260
Curb extensions and speed humps also force traffic to slow down. Visual cues like signs

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.000
are another way to signal to drivers to be even more aware of an impending stop.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:22.880
Even the unpopular roundabout is not only safer than a 4-way or T intersection it also increases traffic flow efficiency.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.760
So now, several cities across the US are rethinking their red-light camera programs.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.260
And Albequerque, New Mexico ended their program in 2011.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:40.440
“The bottom line is that this is a company that is for profit—they’re going to make a profit. And that’s what these cameras do.”

